WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:06.490 --> 00:00:10.760
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: This is probably an appropriate
moment at which to turn to Ian's paper on

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:14.900
the Multispecialty Community Programme, and
invite you to speak to that, Ian.

00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:19.910
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: Thank you very much, Malcolm.
It is almost exactly a year ago today that

00:00:19.910 --> 00:00:24.380
the board discussed and agreed the national
support package for the new care models programme.

00:00:24.380 --> 00:00:30.320
Since then, the vanguards have been getting
on with making change happen. So the snappily

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:35.960
titled multispecialty community provider emerging
care model and contract framework, which I

00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:46.200
have here, is not just a theoretical policy
document; it is assembled from what the 14

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:52.940
different MCP communities are actually doing.
Today we are defining what it means to be

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:58.691
an MCP, and we are setting out the different
options for commissioning and contracting.

00:00:58.691 --> 00:01:03.479
So it is a significant moment, I think, in
the new care model programme, and indeed the

00:01:03.479 --> 00:01:05.729
implementation of the Forward View.

00:01:05.729 --> 00:01:11.049
I will highlight five main points: first,
the connection with sustainability and transformation

00:01:11.049 --> 00:01:18.830
plans. Most of the 44 place centre stage new
models of accountable care provision, whether

00:01:18.830 --> 00:01:24.820
this is in the form of an MCP or the wider
PACS model that also includes all the hospital

00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:32.700
services. So it is clear that the NHS sees
MCPs as an essential transmission mechanism

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:36.679
to help turn STPs into reality.

00:01:36.679 --> 00:01:43.710
Secondly, picking up on the conversation we
have just had about developing and sustaining

00:01:43.710 --> 00:01:52.609
general practice, I think MCPs have a critical
role in this in four ways. The first is supporting

00:01:52.609 --> 00:01:59.969
general practice at scale, including the development
of emerging GP federations; secondly, through

00:01:59.969 --> 00:02:06.680
the additional benefits of combining with
wider community teams; thirdly, in creating

00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:14.750
new career options for clinicians as part
of these bigger integrated entities; and fourthly,

00:02:14.750 --> 00:02:22.190
through the additional oomph that the MCP
can provide in activating and working with

00:02:22.190 --> 00:02:28.260
communities and patients to help tackle demand
within general practice itself.

00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:35.001
Thirdly, I will very briefly outline the core
care model. An MCP is what it says it is:

00:02:35.001 --> 00:02:41.130
it is multi-speciality, the point that James
just made: it is not just about a particular

00:02:41.130 --> 00:02:46.800
set of clinicians; it is community based;
and it is a provider, rather than a new form

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:55.410
of commissioning. It holds and then flexibly
deploys a whole population budget. It depends

00:02:55.410 --> 00:03:01.930
on achieving a deep understanding of its population's
needs, drawing on interoperable records and

00:03:01.930 --> 00:03:08.030
world-class business intelligence. It focuses
on prevention across the whole population.

00:03:08.030 --> 00:03:15.090
It redesigns urgent care as part of a streamlined
urgent and emergency care system. It integrates

00:03:15.090 --> 00:03:22.330
primary and community services in hubs, typically
of the 30,000-50,000 population size, and

00:03:22.330 --> 00:03:27.660
Arvind mentioned the primary care homework
that NAPC is doing on developing exactly that

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:33.610
model. It also provides intensive, proactive
care for small cohorts of patients with the

00:03:33.610 --> 00:03:43.580
very highest needs. In the framework we highlight
examples of what that means in practice, whether

00:03:43.580 --> 00:03:50.530
community volunteering in Sunderland, the
extensivist model in Fylde Coast near Blackpool,

00:03:50.530 --> 00:03:56.370
outpatients redesign in Stockport and Tower
Hamlets or urgent care redesign in Rushcliffe

00:03:56.370 --> 00:03:57.410
in Nottingham.

00:03:57.410 --> 00:04:06.070
Fourthly, I turn to commissioning and contracting.
By far the most important job for an MCP is

00:04:06.070 --> 00:04:12.850
the care redesign, rather than just financial
rewiring. We are really clear: you cannot

00:04:12.850 --> 00:04:20.229
just procure an MCP into being; it requires
very significant change management, engagement

00:04:20.229 --> 00:04:27.240
and development of the workforce and care
pathways over multiple years. However, ultimately,

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:33.009
for all MCPs and indeed new care models to
be sustainable, they need to be commissioned

00:04:33.009 --> 00:04:38.130
so that the financial flows and organisational
governance arrangements actively help rather

00:04:38.130 --> 00:04:42.030
than hinder staff to do the right thing.

00:04:42.030 --> 00:04:48.560
So working intensively with six local systems
and national stakeholders and partners, such

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:54.490
as NHS Improvement, we have been developing
three core approaches to contracting for an

00:04:54.490 --> 00:05:01.650
MCP: first, alliancing arrangements; secondly,
a partially integrated MCP contract; and third,

00:05:01.650 --> 00:05:10.159
a fully integrated MCP contract. In the framework
we then describe the core design principles

00:05:10.159 --> 00:05:16.509
underpinning these, the new budgetary arrangements,
including a whole population budget and new

00:05:16.509 --> 00:05:24.560
pay-for-performance arrangements that synthesise
and redevelop QOF and CQUIN, the different

00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:30.999
options for organisational form, the different
ways of securing the relationship with GPs

00:05:30.999 --> 00:05:38.610
and the procurement method. We will be publishing
a first full draft of the MCP contract in

00:05:38.610 --> 00:05:39.870
September.

00:05:39.870 --> 00:05:46.009
My final point is on wider spread. At the
moment, 8% of the country is currently covered

00:05:46.009 --> 00:05:54.150
by a nationally supported MCP or PACS vanguard.
We expect that this may expand up to around

00:05:54.150 --> 00:06:02.379
25% from 2017/18, supported through a dedicated
new care models funding stream which is linked

00:06:02.379 --> 00:06:05.259
to the STP process.

00:06:05.259 --> 00:06:12.879
So I conclude by commending the fantastic
pioneering work of the MCP vanguards, and

00:06:12.879 --> 00:06:15.550
by inviting the board to endorse the model.

00:06:15.550 --> 00:06:21.379
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much, Ian.
Let me invite the board to raise any comments

00:06:21.379 --> 00:06:23.400
on the paper. Yes please, Noel.

00:06:23.400 --> 00:06:30.030
&gt;&gt;Noel Gordon: Ian, I think this is a tremendous
work you have done on the design. It is really

00:06:30.030 --> 00:06:36.509
comprehensive, it is holistic. In terms of
what patients will see as the benefits of

00:06:36.509 --> 00:06:43.599
the rollout of MCP over time, I sense that
the pace at which you are going to get to

00:06:43.599 --> 00:06:51.460
a quarter of the country is indeed quite accelerated.
However, what I am interested is how we are

00:06:51.460 --> 00:06:58.129
putting in place the measures around what
outcomes patients will see from the roll out

00:06:58.129 --> 00:07:06.639
of MCPs more widely, and whether you think
we need new ways of assessing impact and assessing

00:07:06.639 --> 00:07:13.370
outcome and assessing measures, which will
be another layer of the design if we are indeed

00:07:13.370 --> 00:07:20.220
to measure the benefits that patients see
from MCPs compared to the information that

00:07:20.220 --> 00:07:21.270
we get today.

00:07:21.270 --> 00:07:31.139
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: Thank you, Noel. MCPs and PACS
as whole population models are integrative

00:07:31.139 --> 00:07:37.259
devices; they are about progressing all three
parts of the triple aim: better health and

00:07:37.259 --> 00:07:43.069
wellbeing, better quality care and financial
sustainability. So the national evaluation

00:07:43.069 --> 00:07:51.029
metrics set covers all three of those aspects.
Equally, all of the MCPs and PACS separately

00:07:51.029 --> 00:07:56.050
have their own local core metrics that they
are going after, and they are supported typically

00:07:56.050 --> 00:08:01.970
by £200,000 worth of national funding with
local evaluation work.

00:08:04.889 --> 00:08:12.449
Across those three domains, we have a clear
new indicator set for local communities in

00:08:12.449 --> 00:08:18.219
the form of the CCG improvement and assessment
framework. That sets out a range of different

00:08:18.219 --> 00:08:24.189
metrics which STPs have been using to look
at what the current starting point is in their

00:08:24.189 --> 00:08:30.240
local population, and where they want to improve.
That is feeding through into the work that

00:08:30.240 --> 00:08:34.789
the MCPs and PACS are progressing.

00:08:34.789 --> 00:08:41.440
Specifically on financial sustainability and
the contribution that the MCPs and PACS will

00:08:41.440 --> 00:08:46.990
make to delivering the efficiency savings,
there are two different aspects that all of

00:08:46.990 --> 00:08:54.040
the care models that looking at. One is the
demand moderation element, and that manifests

00:08:54.040 --> 00:08:59.529
itself in reductions in avoidable emergency
admissions but also, in the example of Fylde

00:08:59.529 --> 00:09:06.920
Coast, for example, A&amp;E attendances and outpatient
appointments as well as elective care. Then

00:09:06.920 --> 00:09:12.959
the second is in relation to provider efficiencies
as a result of the synergies you can get from

00:09:12.959 --> 00:09:18.360
creating new, integrated multi-disciplinary
teams. So there is a pretty comprehensive

00:09:18.360 --> 00:09:23.260
metrics set that deals with the whole population
perspective, the patient perspective and indeed

00:09:23.260 --> 00:09:24.850
the tax payer perspective.

00:09:24.850 --> 00:09:26.690
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Victor?

00:09:26.690 --> 00:09:32.750
&gt;&gt;Victor Adebowale: Just following on from
Noel's question: people talk about patients

00:09:32.750 --> 00:09:38.500
and citizens; I am curious about which patients
and which citizens. In that regard, I would

00:09:38.500 --> 00:09:45.720
not mind knowing a bit more about how this
particular model will impact on inequalities,

00:09:45.720 --> 00:09:51.490
mental health - which is an issue that we
have raised - and the promotion of social

00:09:51.490 --> 00:09:53.079
care integration.

00:09:53.079 --> 00:10:03.910
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: Thank you, Victor. So I think
there are some really great examples in terms

00:10:03.910 --> 00:10:11.070
of the design and the initial adoption in
relation to each of those areas. However,

00:10:11.070 --> 00:10:18.269
there is also more work to be done within
the vanguards as they move from their current

00:10:18.269 --> 00:10:23.920
plans towards achieving full maturity in each
of those different elements. I will just touch

00:10:23.920 --> 00:10:30.300
briefly on the design potential, and then
some of the current examples we are seeing.

00:10:30.300 --> 00:10:36.110
So one of the purposes of creating a whole
population budget that combines existing primary

00:10:36.110 --> 00:10:41.459
medical services spend, community services
spend and some of these other budgets, into

00:10:41.459 --> 00:10:49.500
a larger and more resilient provider entity
is that it creates the opportunity to redesign

00:10:49.500 --> 00:10:54.290
care services around what are genuinely the
different needs of different populations.

00:10:54.290 --> 00:11:00.130
So if you look at what, for example, the South
Somerset PACS has been doing, they identify

00:11:00.130 --> 00:11:06.139
that something like 50% of their spend is
on 4% of their patients. If you have a single

00:11:06.139 --> 00:11:11.819
bigger budget for all of those out-of-hospital
services, they are able to target and deploy

00:11:11.819 --> 00:11:18.230
that budget far more effectively at basically
intensive services on the patients that have

00:11:18.230 --> 00:11:24.060
the very highest need, and indeed looking
at particular communities and neighbours within

00:11:24.060 --> 00:11:29.589
their geography where the services currently
atomised, are unable to provide the kind of

00:11:29.589 --> 00:11:33.020
high dose intervention that we would all look
to try and see.

00:11:33.020 --> 00:11:38.180
We have got some really good examples where
vanguards are specifically tackling equalities.

00:11:38.180 --> 00:11:44.949
We are working with the Institute of Health
Equity and Sir Michael Marmot in two MCPs,

00:11:44.949 --> 00:11:50.490
in Cheshire West and in Tower Hamlets, to
be able to improve the granularity of the

00:11:50.490 --> 00:11:56.400
dataset of how they tackle inequalities and
what they do by way of their impact. I think

00:11:56.400 --> 00:12:00.819
all of them, all of the MCPs I have seen - and
I have read all of their value propositions

00:12:00.819 --> 00:12:06.860
and visited many of them - have built into
their model tackling inequalities at their

00:12:06.860 --> 00:12:13.750
core. Have they all made substantial progress
around that yet? Some, but there is more work

00:12:13.750 --> 00:12:14.750
to do.

00:12:14.750 --> 00:12:19.639
On mental health, we are similarly seeing
the vanguards integrate physical health in

00:12:19.639 --> 00:12:25.449
the community with mental health in the community.
I think this is an increasing journey. We

00:12:25.449 --> 00:12:32.649
had a few, such as in Hampshire: the work
that the GPs in Gosport and Fareham, connecting

00:12:32.649 --> 00:12:38.100
with Southern Healthcare, have been doing
around creating integrated teams that include

00:12:38.100 --> 00:12:43.209
mental health for the first time. As most
of our MCPs have been going through their

00:12:43.209 --> 00:12:47.740
journey of understanding their population
needs, they are increasingly looking to provide

00:12:47.740 --> 00:12:52.810
that integration. I was up in Birmingham on
Friday talking to some of the people in Dudley,

00:12:52.810 --> 00:12:59.879
and indeed in the MERIT wider mental health
vanguard, and if you look at the service specification,

00:12:59.879 --> 00:13:05.250
the prospectus that Dudley has produced, over
the last six months they have decided to include

00:13:05.250 --> 00:13:10.850
mental health as a core part of what they
are doing. We can see a similar journey in

00:13:10.850 --> 00:13:16.170
relation to social care integration. The framework
highlights in particular the excellent work

00:13:16.170 --> 00:13:20.389
that Sunderland are doing around re-ablement
services.

00:13:20.389 --> 00:13:24.459
So I think there are lots of green shoots.
This is built into the design, but much more

00:13:24.459 --> 00:13:26.960
to do around systematic execution.

00:13:26.960 --> 00:13:29.930
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Thanks, Ian. John?

00:13:29.930 --> 00:13:35.500
&gt;&gt;John Burn: Ian, this is a pretty elegant
piece of work, and I am very impressed by

00:13:35.500 --> 00:13:39.860
it. I am just thinking about how GPs will
react to it. In particular, Arvind was encouraging

00:13:39.860 --> 00:13:44.560
us that we are going to see an upsurge in
recruitment to general practice, but my colleagues

00:13:44.560 --> 00:13:48.639
in general practice tell me there is a sort
of generational shift away from being partners

00:13:48.639 --> 00:13:56.930
towards contractual GPs. What is your feel
for the different generations of GPs' reaction

00:13:56.930 --> 00:14:01.470
to this? One senses perhaps this could be
seen as a very positive move for those younger

00:14:01.470 --> 00:14:06.140
GPs, who do not want to be partners in small
practices but would rather be part of a bigger

00:14:06.140 --> 00:14:08.550
organisation. Is that your take as well?

00:14:08.550 --> 00:14:13.769
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: The point I would emphasise above
all others is that we have developed this

00:14:13.769 --> 00:14:21.139
as a voluntary set of options for GPs. We
have said if practices want to continue to

00:14:21.139 --> 00:14:27.120
work as they currently are, whether this is
individual, small partnerships or salaried

00:14:27.120 --> 00:14:32.110
GPs in their current practices, then that
is fine, that is great.

00:14:32.110 --> 00:14:37.240
However, what we are trying to do is go with
the grain of what the profession itself has

00:14:37.240 --> 00:14:43.779
wanted over multiple years. I personally have
spent a chunk of the last 20 years of working

00:14:43.779 --> 00:14:50.930
in the NHS in engaging with general practice
and GP leaders around their aspirations for

00:14:50.930 --> 00:14:57.089
creating a more sensible set of provisions,
a fatter version of general practice in terms

00:14:57.089 --> 00:15:02.759
of the scope of services. For decades, the
aspirations of the profession have been to

00:15:02.759 --> 00:15:09.199
join up more effectively their practice nurses
with community matrons, with district nurses,

00:15:09.199 --> 00:15:13.540
with domiciliary care nurses, in order that
they can redesign care around what patients

00:15:13.540 --> 00:15:19.860
need and what makes most sense professionally,
rather than the legacy of different institutions.

00:15:19.860 --> 00:15:25.180
So what we are doing here is we are trying
to create a set of different voluntary options

00:15:25.180 --> 00:15:30.279
for clinicians to work together in a way that
is institutionally blind. We are seeing in

00:15:30.279 --> 00:15:36.029
our areas a huge groundswell of interest in
doing that, because it is based on their professional

00:15:36.029 --> 00:15:37.029
values.

00:15:37.029 --> 00:15:43.810
Now, within that, inevitably if people are
content with their current arrangements, then

00:15:43.810 --> 00:15:48.540
we need to make sure that the arrangements
that are devised here suit different circumstances.

00:15:48.540 --> 00:15:53.490
That is one of the reasons why we are saying
there is not a single contracting model that

00:15:53.490 --> 00:15:59.680
is the right answer. We have developed here
not definitive national policy that says this

00:15:59.680 --> 00:16:04.300
is the only way of doing it, but three options
that are emerging from our intense engagement

00:16:04.300 --> 00:16:09.279
with local systems, including the conversations
that are happening between the MCP and the

00:16:09.279 --> 00:16:17.800
GPs in Whitstable, in Dudley, in Greater Manchester,
in Wakefield. So I hope we have a set of attractive,

00:16:17.800 --> 00:16:24.100
flexible options, and we will see whether
it does the job for GPs as part of the wider

00:16:24.100 --> 00:16:25.390
package that Arvind has described.

00:16:25.390 --> 00:16:28.649
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Thank you. I have Wendy and
David. Wendy?

00:16:28.649 --> 00:16:33.740
&gt;&gt;Wendy Becker: Yeah, thank you Ian. I understand
the GP perspective. I want to just go back

00:16:33.740 --> 00:16:39.829
a little bit to the patient perspective, though.
As a sort of average person on the street,

00:16:39.829 --> 00:16:45.681
I get bigger, fewer, better integrated practices,
why that makes sense. However, for me as a

00:16:45.681 --> 00:16:50.450
typical patient, why am I not losing choice
and why am I better off because of this?

00:16:50.450 --> 00:16:58.490
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: So I would pick up on two things
from that. The first is on your point around

00:16:58.490 --> 00:17:08.780
patient choice. As MCPs come into being, the
MCPs themselves realise that they need to

00:17:08.780 --> 00:17:13.620
find ways of safeguarding the ability to move
from one practice to another practice that

00:17:13.620 --> 00:17:19.730
will be contained within the MCP, and we are
exploring that with the MCPs.

00:17:19.730 --> 00:17:25.770
The other thing that we need to do, and that
they are also convinced by, is that there

00:17:25.770 --> 00:17:32.120
is a set of NHS constitution rights for patients
to have choice of other services, whether

00:17:32.120 --> 00:17:37.870
it is mental health services, whether it is
elective care, and we will be working with

00:17:37.870 --> 00:17:43.970
the MCPs and the PACS to make sure that those
constitution rights, as reaffirmed in the

00:17:43.970 --> 00:17:47.750
Forward View, are then systematically delivered
for their patients.

00:17:47.750 --> 00:17:52.340
For patients with the very highest needs,
who might be most dissatisfied with current

00:17:52.340 --> 00:17:58.919
services - we are coming on later today to
the learning disability programme - they are

00:17:58.919 --> 00:18:04.240
increasingly offered integrated personal budgets,
so that they can design for themselves the

00:18:04.240 --> 00:18:10.120
services they want. Tower Hamlets is an MCP
vanguard that is also one of our integrated

00:18:10.120 --> 00:18:14.410
personalised commissioning pioneers, that
is beginning to show how you combine those

00:18:14.410 --> 00:18:16.080
different service together.

00:18:16.080 --> 00:18:22.620
The second theme I wanted to pick up on is
on continuity of care for patients. So the

00:18:22.620 --> 00:18:29.090
research over many years shows that patients
often like small practices because of the

00:18:29.090 --> 00:18:37.130
relational continuity. One of the main things
that the MCPs, and indeed the GP federations,

00:18:37.130 --> 00:18:45.280
are looking at is how scale can both continue
that continuity but provide a better, more

00:18:45.280 --> 00:18:51.860
joined-up service for the people with the
highest needs. So actually, if you are an

00:18:51.860 --> 00:18:57.640
85 year-old person with multiple comorbidities,
you might be able to see your GP but only

00:18:57.640 --> 00:19:02.720
for your traditional ten minute consultation,
whereas if I take you to the Fylde Coast to

00:19:02.720 --> 00:19:07.080
the extensivist model there, you might be
part of a group of patients that has been

00:19:07.080 --> 00:19:14.350
identified as needing intensive assessment
and support, home visits, the local pharmacist,

00:19:14.350 --> 00:19:19.890
actually looking at polypharmacy and dealing
with you holistically in a way that you just

00:19:19.890 --> 00:19:21.549
cannot do in a ten minute consultation.

00:19:21.549 --> 00:19:25.679
So one of the reasons why some of the GPs,
and indeed geriatricians and pharmacists,

00:19:25.679 --> 00:19:31.000
are really attracted to these models is it
can enable them more time to have the more

00:19:31.000 --> 00:19:33.940
intense, continuous relationship with the
patients that need it most.

00:19:33.940 --> 00:19:35.150
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: David?

00:19:35.150 --> 00:19:42.409
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Okay, thank you Malcolm.
One thing to say is that these proposals were

00:19:42.409 --> 00:19:47.470
given a good workout in the Commissioning
Committee, and I recommended to the board

00:19:47.470 --> 00:19:53.600
there. So important to recognise this is not
the only point of challenge and governance.

00:19:53.600 --> 00:20:04.039
I think, Ian, my question is really about
implementation. So if I look at the ten essential

00:20:04.039 --> 00:20:10.350
jobs in creating an MCP, you are changing
people, process, technology; it is a very

00:20:10.350 --> 00:20:18.120
fundamental change, and it is a very significant
financial change as well in the way the flows

00:20:18.120 --> 00:20:25.191
work and so on. As you have said yourself,
the establishment of the MCP is not the issue;

00:20:25.191 --> 00:20:31.830
the issue is redesigning the care pathways
and processes to be able to deliver better

00:20:31.830 --> 00:20:33.179
support for patients.

00:20:33.179 --> 00:20:39.000
So with that in mind, that it is a big change,
it is very important and I suspect, given

00:20:39.000 --> 00:20:46.260
in STPs this is going to be a model that people
will want to adopt more broadly, two questions.

00:20:46.260 --> 00:20:55.289
One is: is there enough change capacity and
capability, both in NHS England and in the

00:20:55.289 --> 00:20:58.110
system, to support delivery?

00:20:58.110 --> 00:21:05.520
Secondly, there have been some problems in
establishing big, new innovative ways of working;

00:21:05.520 --> 00:21:12.690
for example, UnitingCare would be one. How
do we avoid some of the mistakes that we have

00:21:12.690 --> 00:21:16.600
made in the past? What assurance can you give
the board on those two?

00:21:16.600 --> 00:21:24.820
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: Thank you, David. So on your
first point around whether there is enough

00:21:24.820 --> 00:21:31.770
support, I think that becomes a really critical
issue when we get beyond the vanguards. So

00:21:31.770 --> 00:21:37.430
we were having the conversation yesterday;
I will be working very closely with Mathew

00:21:37.430 --> 00:21:44.210
and NHSI around how we can provide joined
up support; not just to support the transformation

00:21:44.210 --> 00:21:53.230
through MCPs but the delivery of STPs more
widely. The support we provide for accountable

00:21:53.230 --> 00:21:59.670
care models is part of that wider support,
and we recognise that there is a big job to

00:21:59.670 --> 00:22:08.390
be done to gear up local systems, and indeed
NHS England's own regional and local teams,

00:22:08.390 --> 00:22:12.730
working hand-in-glove with NHSI and other
partners. Because this is not just about NHS

00:22:12.730 --> 00:22:20.649
England and NHSI, it is also about PAG and
HEE and NHS Digital, critically.

00:22:20.649 --> 00:22:28.240
On the UnitingCare - and in fact, thank you
for raising that - I think there are four

00:22:28.240 --> 00:22:34.340
ways in which this will be different from
UnitingCare. So first is the very design of

00:22:34.340 --> 00:22:38.399
the programme with the vanguards, that we
are starting first with the care model changes

00:22:38.399 --> 00:22:42.650
rather than thinking this is just a procurement
exercise. This is about local leaders that

00:22:42.650 --> 00:22:48.100
are getting on, driving and creating the actual
teams and making the service models work.

00:22:48.100 --> 00:22:55.110
Secondly, there is the design of the contracts
themselves: the population basis, the service

00:22:55.110 --> 00:22:59.950
scope, the financial flows. We do not have
the complexity of just carving out an over

00:22:59.950 --> 00:23:03.669
65 cohort. There is a set of things that are
being done very differently here compared

00:23:03.669 --> 00:23:05.730
to that Cambridgeshire contract.

00:23:05.730 --> 00:23:11.050
Thirdly, there is some detailed, dedicated
work that my team have been taking forward

00:23:11.050 --> 00:23:18.399
with Paul on the assessment of risk, and how
that financial risk is managed and what mitigations

00:23:18.399 --> 00:23:21.070
need to put in place around that.

00:23:21.070 --> 00:23:28.299
Then fourthly there will be, for each of the
vanguards as a learning process, a joint oversight

00:23:28.299 --> 00:23:35.480
between NHS England and NHSI of each of the
procurements that will be occurring in the

00:23:35.480 --> 00:23:42.940
six intensive development sites. We will sign
those off nationally, not just locally, and

00:23:42.940 --> 00:23:44.270
as part of a learning community.

00:23:44.270 --> 00:23:50.140
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Okay. So I think the change
capacity and capability, what I heard you

00:23:50.140 --> 00:23:54.310
say is, 'That is work in progress.' It is
clearly critical, and maybe Malcolm, that

00:23:54.310 --> 00:24:01.620
is something in due course the board can return
to, because it is absolutely central to our

00:24:01.620 --> 00:24:04.330
ability to effect changes in a safe and timely
way.

00:24:04.330 --> 00:24:11.399
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Just building on that, I
think one of the distinguishing features of

00:24:11.399 --> 00:24:18.049
UnitingCare was the vehicle that was used
as a LLP. I notice in paragraph 16 of your

00:24:18.049 --> 00:24:25.850
cover paper you talk about different vehicles,
you do not include a LLP. Or do you?

00:24:25.850 --> 00:24:33.950
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: Yes, we absolutely do include
the possibility of LLP, not least because

00:24:33.950 --> 00:24:39.030
some of the emerging provider forms may come
out of, for example, general practice and

00:24:39.030 --> 00:24:43.990
GP federations. I think the issue we have
is that we do not want to end up creating

00:24:43.990 --> 00:24:49.490
an inadvertent bias where we say, in order
to mitigate the risk, we are pushing you towards

00:24:49.490 --> 00:24:54.510
a particular form of provider - for example,
a statutory NHS provider - where we know of

00:24:54.510 --> 00:25:01.299
course there is lots of risk associated with
financial management in those forms. I think

00:25:01.299 --> 00:25:06.011
the bigger question is about to what extent
the risk is clear and understood, and there

00:25:06.011 --> 00:25:11.309
are effective ways of ensuring that it is
owned by the provider, including, for example,

00:25:11.309 --> 00:25:15.380
the issue of parent company guarantees or
balance sheet strengths. Those are exactly

00:25:15.380 --> 00:25:23.731
the kind of tests that the joint work Paul
and I are doing will be assessing and looking

00:25:23.731 --> 00:25:24.731
at.

00:25:24.731 --> 00:25:26.330
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: I think that is very reassuring.
I think the second point - I am building again

00:25:26.330 --> 00:25:31.059
on David's question to you - I suppose is
the pace at which we crystallise these models.

00:25:31.059 --> 00:25:37.310
I think this is just so important to get right,
and working with the six areas is a critical

00:25:37.310 --> 00:25:43.720
way of developing the three potential organisational
models and contracts. However, alongside this

00:25:43.720 --> 00:25:50.320
we see the STPs developing at pace. For me,
one of the critical components of the development

00:25:50.320 --> 00:25:56.549
of STPs will be the MCPs evolving beyond the
vanguards and evolving in other areas where

00:25:56.549 --> 00:26:03.039
presently they are at an early stage. So do
you see these two work streams converging

00:26:03.039 --> 00:26:08.519
satisfactorily so as to provide a clear model
that can be used within the STP frameworks?

00:26:08.519 --> 00:26:17.029
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: Absolutely, I think so. If I
can give you an example about the complementarity

00:26:17.029 --> 00:26:22.909
between the STPs and the new care models:
if you take an area such as Greater Manchester,

00:26:22.909 --> 00:26:29.610
Greater Manchester has the wider plan, the
STP for its geography. Within that it has

00:26:29.610 --> 00:26:36.710
a whole different array of implementation
mechanisms and methods, including the development

00:26:36.710 --> 00:26:42.450
of new forms of acute collaboration, for example,
Salford and partners; the work that is going

00:26:42.450 --> 00:26:49.620
on in Central Manchester, the work that the
Christie is doing with partners in creating

00:26:49.620 --> 00:26:58.010
basically a cancer scaled model across GM.
We have also then separately got the development

00:26:58.010 --> 00:27:04.990
of the MCP vanguard in Stockport, and indeed
across the whole of GM. Tracy Vell, the local

00:27:04.990 --> 00:27:10.760
medical committee secretary, has been leading
the work to develop ten local care organisations

00:27:10.760 --> 00:27:19.289
which is a GM version of MCPs, and these GM
sees as ways of then giving effect to what

00:27:19.289 --> 00:27:22.990
the STP is looking to try and deliver.

00:27:22.990 --> 00:27:28.880
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Good, okay. That is very
reassuring. For me, the Holy Grail of this

00:27:28.880 --> 00:27:34.490
is actually the contract and getting that
right; the commissioning and the contract,

00:27:34.490 --> 00:27:39.135
and getting the crystallisation, working through
with the six areas that you mention in the

00:27:39.135 --> 00:27:46.760
paper but on a programme that then allows
greater spread as we move into implementation

00:27:46.760 --> 00:27:47.760
of the STPs.

00:27:47.760 --> 00:27:54.490
So I think I can take from what you say a
good assurance from that. May I raise further

00:27:54.490 --> 00:27:59.779
questions for you? No? Well, can we thank
you? I know you and your team have put an

00:27:59.779 --> 00:28:04.470
enormous amount of work into this, working
closely with the vanguards to come up with

00:28:04.470 --> 00:28:07.309
quite exciting new models of care. So thank
you for that.

